  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 853 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nIsolated snow showers are possible in the mountains overnight \nwith minor accumulations. Other than that most locations will be\ndry and that continues into tomorrow. Given so much moisture \nand limited sun today, patchy fog is certainly possible in the \nfavorable spots tonight. Tomorrow will be partly cloudy with \ntemperatures 5-10 degrees below normal. This break will be short\nlived, as another low pressure works across the region on \nWednesday. While moisture remains unimpressive with this system,\na few showers and thunderstorms are expected Wednesday \nafternoon and overnight. Given the saturated soil there is \nconcern for any additional rainfall. As of now the precipitation\namounts are minor in comparison to the last event. Although \nthunderstorms could produce the rates for localized issues. We \nwill continue to monitor this situation. The mountains should \npick up another couple inches of snow too before the \nprecipitation comes to an end Thursday morning. \n\nModels continue to sell broad high pressure building across the \nregion to close out the week. This should help streamflows and soils \nto dry out. Temperatures will remain cooler than normal, but a \nslight warm up is expected this weekend ahead of a potential pattern \nshift early next week.",
